<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arikesari Maravarman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Koon Pandiyan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arikesari Maravarman was a king of the early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in the later 7th century. Shaiva tradition holds that the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> converted him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, an episode associated with the rise of the Bhakti movement in Tamil country. He extended Pandya power against neighbouring rulers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arikesari Maravarman, also known as Parankusa, succeeded Seliyan Sendan, or Jayantavarman, and was most probably his son, though this is not certain. His name appears as Arikesari Maravarman in the Velvikkudi Grant and the Smaller Chinnamanur Plates, and as Arikesari Parankusa in the Larger Chinnamanur Plates. Historians, among them K. A. Nilakanta Sastri, T. V. Sadasiva Pandarathar and Noburu Karashima, give different dates for his reign. He was succeeded by his son Kocchadaiyan Ranadhira.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign probably saw a marked rise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> power and prestige, and the beginning of Pandya rivalry with the Pallavas in the north of the Tamil country. According to the Velvikkudi Grant he won battles at Pali, Nelveli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a>; apart from Uraiyur, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>, these places cannot be identified with certainty. The Larger Chinnamanur Plates credit him with victories at Nelveli and Sankaramangai, the latter over the Pallavas, and say he ruined the Paravars of the south-east coast who would not submit and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Keralas</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>, several times, once imprisoning their king with his relatives and warriors.</p><p>He seems at first to have joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> against the Pallavas. When the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-i">Vikramaditya I</a> drove the Pallava king Parameswara I from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> and camped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraiyur">Uraiyur</a>, he probably made an alliance with Arikesari there. The historian K. M. Venkata Ramaiah, however, records that at Nelveli the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> defeated the Chalukyas under Vikramaditya I, which suggests shifting hostilities rather than a stable alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Arikesari is known to have performed the Hiranyagarbha and Tulabhara rituals several times. He is sometimes identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king Nedumaran, or Koon Pandya, who in legend married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> princess Mangayarkkarasi, herself a Shaivite saint, and was himself later regarded as a saint. The commentary on the Iraiyanar Ahapporul names a king Arikesari with many of the same battles, and the historian Venkayya took the two to be one ruler, but K. A. N. Sastri rejected the identification, arguing that such a rhetorical work gave its saintly hero the deeds of many Pandya kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arikesari Maravarman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Arikesari Maravarman</b>, according to Shaiva tradition, was converted from Jainism by the saint Sambandar, and the Bhakti movement of the Nayanars and Alvars flourished under Pandya patronage.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-pandyan-empire">First Pandyan Empire</a></li></ul></section>
